    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d69">

                <pb facs="0218" n="94" sameAs="frus:frus1881/pg94"/>

                <head>No. 69.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Blaine to Mr. Nuñez del
                    Prado</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1881-06-14"><hi rendition="#italic">June</hi> 14,
                        1881</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the receipt
                    of your excellency’s note of February 1 last, setting forth the reasons which
                    animate and control the Government of Bolivia in resisting to the last extremity
                    the encroachments of the Chilian Government.</p>
                <p>The people of this country have watched, not without the deepest interest, the
                    continued struggle between the three nations on the Pacific, whose institutions
                    and forms of government are allied to their own, in the sincere hope that the
                    differences of whatever nature which engendered the present conflict might be
                    amicably and satisfactorily adjusted, and the perpetuity and prosperity of those
                    republics guaranteed.</p>
                <pb facs="0219" n="95" xml:id="pg95"/>
                <p>Still cherishing this confident hope, I bid you accept, sir, in the name of the
                    Government and people of the United States the assurances of my highest
                    consideration.</p>
                <closer><signed>JAMES G. BLAINE.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
